# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the build OUTPUT directory
# (public/) -- build.py copies it there automatically.
#
# Apex-to-www canonicalization (smartgeeks.ca -> www.smartgeeks.ca) is
# deliberately NOT handled here. Confirmed against a real Cloudflare Pages
# build log: the _redirects file only accepts a relative path as the
# source of a rule, so a host-conditional redirect like
# "https://smartgeeks.ca/* https://www.smartgeeks.ca/:splat 301!" is parsed
# as invalid and silently skipped -- Cloudflare logs "Only relative URLs
# are allowed" and drops the line, so it would never actually run.
#
# The correct way to canonicalize apex -> www on Cloudflare is a
# zone-level Redirect Rule (Rules -> Redirect Rules in the dashboard, on
# the smartgeeks.ca zone) -- see DEPLOYMENT.md "Custom domain setup" for
# the exact steps. That mechanism operates on incoming hostname before
# Pages ever sees the request, which is what a domain-level redirect
# actually requires.
#
# This file is kept (rather than deleted) as the place same-path,
# same-host redirects belong if any are ever needed -- e.g.
#   /old-page/*  /new-page/:splat  301

# --- Retired Google Ads landing pages (2026-09-01 policy recovery pass) ---
# These four pages were built exclusively as Google Ads destinations for
# repair-service campaigns that are PROHIBITED under Google's current
# Third-Party Consumer Technical Support policy (see
# CURRENT-POLICY-SOURCE-REGISTER.md). They duplicated content already on
# the real service pages, so each one 301-redirects to its closest real,
# organic service page. See URL-MIGRATION-MAP.md for rationale per URL.
/landing/computer-repair-surrey-ads/*      /services/                 301
/landing/laptop-repair-surrey-ads/*        /services/laptop-repair/   301
/landing/mac-repair-surrey-ads/*           /services/                 301
/landing/gaming-console-repair-surrey-ads/*  /services/gaming-console-repair/  301
